---
import methodDiagram from '../assets/how-the-tracker-works.inline.svg?raw';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ToolEmbed from '../components/ToolEmbed.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Data Centres Sovereignty Tracker"
  description="The Data Centres Sovereignty Tracker: a living atlas of the infrastructure behind AI in Australia, and the public debate forming around it. Data centres, mines, refineries, energy and water, and the lines of ownership running through them."
  ogImage="/images/og-sovereignties.png"
  dataset={{
    name: "Data Centres Sovereignty Tracker",
    description: "A live, openly maintained map of the infrastructure behind AI in Australia: data centres, rare-earth mines, refineries, and the energy and water they draw, with ownership, approval pathways, and the public contestation forming around each site.",
    keywords: ["AI sovereignty", "data centres", "critical minerals", "AI infrastructure", "Australia", "compute", "rare earths"],
    spatialCoverage: "Australia",
  }}
>
  <main class="container sov">

    <header class="sov-header">
      <p class="sov-eyebrow">Civic Interplay / Tracker</p>
      <h1>Data Centres Sovereignty Tracker</h1>
      <p class="sov-intro">
        <strong>A <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">living atlas</a> of infrastructural AI in Australia.</strong> Site by site, it
        records who owns the compute, what each facility draws in power and water, how it was approved, where it has
        been contested, and which supply chains it depends on. The tracker is open: the
        <a href="https://github.com/civic-interplay/ai-sovereignties" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">code base is on GitHub</a>, alongside
        <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/sheets" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">summary data sheets</a> for each State and Territory, a
        <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/glossary" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">glossary</a>, a
        <a href="https://github.com/civic-interplay/ai-sovereignties/blob/main/docs/METHODOLOGY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">methods statement</a> and a
        <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/news" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">news feed</a> (<a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/news/feed.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">RSS</a>).
      </p>
    </header>

    <ToolEmbed
      title="Data Centres Sovereignty Tracker"
      src="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/"
      openLabel="Open the map in a new tab"
      height="820px"
    />
    <p class="sov-note">
      <em>If the map does not load, your browser may be blocking the embedded site.
      <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Open it in a new tab</a> instead.</em>
    </p>

    <section class="sov-section sov-about">
      <p>
        This project examines data centre approvals in Australia as a <strong>contested terrain</strong>, reflecting
        competing versions of agency and sovereignty that are expressed by place-based communities.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tracker is the <strong>first practice-based methodological expansion of <a href="/reading/">civic AI</a></strong> by researcher and digital
        strategist Dr Sarah Barns (RMIT University). It uses AI skills and methods to <strong>make flows of public value
        more accountable and legible</strong>, while also examining the extent to which current practices of data
        governance support the goals of transparency and democratic accountability.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <strong>core proposition</strong> being tested here is that digital tools, including AI agents, can be used to
        expand and democratise how public policies are understood and interpreted for their impacts on places over time.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, their effectiveness also depends on a set of core digital capabilities and services that require
        concurrent investment for this methodology to be practical and meaningful. These include: <strong>open and machine-readable
        data infrastructures</strong>, <strong>commitment to open and transparent reporting</strong> around public value
        and accountability, and <strong>digital literacies around platform dependencies</strong> and their consequences
        for digital agency, including AI agency.
      </p>
      <h2>Civic AI applied to data centre approvals</h2>
      <p>
        Combined with a place-based approach, the tools of civic AI are being adopted to interrogate how public policy
        frameworks are adapting to a <strong>&lsquo;super cycle&rsquo; wave of infrastructural AI investment</strong> in
        Australia.
      </p>
      <p>Key focus areas for this Data Centre Tracker include:</p>
      <ul class="sov-registers">
        <li>comparative levels of <strong>data transparency</strong> across states and territories;</li>
        <li><strong>forms of governance</strong> practised in the approvals process;</li>
        <li>levels of data transparency <strong>relative to purported claims</strong>; and</li>
        <li>expressions of <strong>civic agency</strong> evidenced through the formal public approvals process.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The methodology operates as a form of <strong>recursive listening</strong>, where sites of urban transformation
        are read as informational archives, structured and interpreted through distinctive methods for listening,
        attention, interpretation and gathering over time (including information gathering).
      </p>
      <p>
        Attention to this dimension of urban transformation is designed to foster greater awareness of the role and value
        of informational transparency and accountability in shaping good governance and decision making on behalf of
        current and future generations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>Mapping AI sovereignties</h2>
      <p>
        Core to this tracker is the recognition that <strong>AI sovereignty is mobilised as a highly normative
        term</strong> that supports the accumulation of value, public and private, around investment in AI
        infrastructure globally.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, AI sovereignty as a framework mobilised to support investment in AI infrastructure and skills also
        operates to <strong>undermine existing practices of sovereignty</strong>, including place-based sovereignties:
        Country-centred and local community sovereignties.
      </p>
      <p>
        Negotiations over contested versions of sovereignty occur <strong>at the site of data centre approvals</strong>,
        where local and state-based planning regimes approve large-scale data centres supported by vast global investment
        flows, as well as national sovereignty actors in the form of sovereign wealth funds and superannuation funds.
      </p>
      <p>
        National policies also advance national sovereignty goals for digital infrastructure through public policy
        settings including, in Australia, the <strong>Foreign Investment Review Board (FIRB)</strong> and
        <strong>spending on public compute</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each of these layers reflects different values towards the nature of digital sovereignty, and in particular AI
        sovereignty, and each is a focus for this tracker.
      </p>
      <img
        class="sov-chart"
        src="/images/ai-sovereignty-timeline.svg"
        alt="Line chart of global news coverage mentioning 'sovereign AI' or 'AI sovereignty', rising from near-zero in 2022 to a peak in 2026."
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <p class="sov-chart-credit">
        Global news coverage mentioning &ldquo;sovereign AI&rdquo; or &ldquo;AI sovereignty&rdquo;, relative volume, 2022
        to 2026. Source: GDELT.
        <a href="https://app.notion.com/p/38e18df0b77581f5a06cd1b03bfd4707" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Data and method</a> in the
        <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/AI-Sovereignties-project-explainer-38d18df0b77581ab8465dcb65a6628ad" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">project explainer</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section" id="methodology">
      <h2>Key findings and methods</h2>
      <p>
        In Australia, <strong>dependency on global cloud providers is a condition of participation</strong> in AI
        infrastructure. This is
        reflected in the reliance on the US frontier model developer Anthropic in the development of this civic AI
        methodology (noting that alternative models are also available).
      </p>
      <p>
        Tracking the approvals of data centres in Australia (<a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">view the map</a>), the project identifies
        <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/trends" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">key trends</a> in both approvals and data transparency, alongside
        <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/sheets" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">summary data sheets</a> by state and territory and
        <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/news" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">news reportage</a>, and the full
        <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/Contestation-sovereignty-trackers-full-session-38d18df0b77581f697dfe059a69b454d" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">contestation record</a>
        in Notion. A summary of the methodology adopted for the project is below, with an
        accompanying <a href="https://datacentres.civicinterplay.io/glossary" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">glossary of key terms</a>,
        the <a href="https://github.com/civic-interplay/ai-sovereignties/blob/main/docs/FACT-CHECKING-GUIDE.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">fact-checking protocol</a> used for human verification, a <a href="https://github.com/civic-interplay/ai-sovereignties" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">GitHub repository</a> and a
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21026429" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">DOI</a> for citation purposes.
      </p>
      <figure class="sov-figure">
        <div class="sov-diagram" set:html={methodDiagram} />
        <figcaption>
          Every box is marked as done by a person or by an AI agent.
          <a href="/images/how-the-tracker-works.svg" download>Download the diagram (SVG)</a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>Platform dependencies</h3>
      <p>The tracker depends on parts of the stack it maps, and it records that dependency.</p>
      <ul class="sov-deps">
        <li>
          <strong>Anthropic.</strong> Claude is the model behind the research agents. Sites in the tracker listed as
          hosting, or planned to host, Anthropic&rsquo;s models in Australia include
          <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/33918df0b775813db5cccb163b3f15ef" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">AWS Asia Pacific (Sydney)</a>,
          <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/32a18df0b775812f975ce9c8a8106691" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Amazon&rsquo;s Sydney and Melbourne investment</a>,
          <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/38d18df0b775816da977d4ce2d3c1ed3" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Google Cloud Sydney and Melbourne</a>, and the proposed
          <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/3c818df0b77581eb8935ccb900394d0e" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Western Downs Digital Park</a> near Dalby (lease subject to FIRB approval).
        </li>
        <li><strong>Notion</strong> holds the database. All data can be exported at any time.</li>
        <li><strong>MCP and public APIs</strong> connect the tools.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloudflare and Mapbox</strong> provide hosting and base maps.</li>
        <li><strong>Factiva</strong> provides licensed news through an RMIT licence. It is read by hand, never scraped.</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub</strong> holds the code, data and documentation. Each version is deposited on <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21026429" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Zenodo with a DOI</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>Accounting for compute costs</h2>
      <p>
        The project also runs a tracker of the reported computational costs of the research, in the
        <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/10618c7776a749fb8051ee4e8d1df570?v=4b15487e90c245358d7cae4d7c85cd89" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Cost of Compute tracker</a>,
        and the same records are saved in the <a href="https://github.com/civic-interplay/ai-sovereignties/blob/main/docs/COMPUTE-LOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">GitHub repository</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>Related reports and presentations</h2>
      <ul class="sov-registers">
        <li><a href="/submission/">Submission to the Senate inquiry into AI and data centres</a> (1 September 2026), with fourteen recommendations</li>
        <li><a href="/data-centres-map/">Mapping Australian Data Centres</a>: the story behind the map</li>
        <li>Barns, S. (2026). <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21980637" target="_blank" rel="noopener external"><em>Rethinking the AI stack from the ground up, through recursive monitoring methods</em></a>. Conference paper, Data for Policy 2026, Barcelona, 8&ndash;10 September 2026.</li>
        <li>American Association of Geographers Annual Meeting, San Francisco, April 2026 (<a href="/aag-barns-2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">slides, PDF</a>)</li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21026429" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Dataset on Zenodo</a>: every version of the tracker data, citable with a DOI</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>Citation and licence</h2>
      <p class="sov-cite">
        Sarah Barns, <em>A living atlas of contesting and curating AI sovereignties (Australian view)</em>, Civic Interplay, 2026.
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21026429" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21026429</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        The methodology, classification and written analysis are licensed under
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">CC BY 4.0</a>. The underlying records are compiled from
        public sources and cited per entry; the facts themselves carry no licence claim. Sourcing and classification are
        set out in the <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/AI-Sovereignties-project-explainer-38d18df0b77581ab8465dcb65a6628ad" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">project explainer</a>
        and, in fuller form, the <a href="https://github.com/civic-interplay/ai-sovereignties/blob/main/docs/METHODOLOGY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">methodology statement</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For research partnerships or use of the data in publication,
        <a href="https://cur.org.au/people/dr-sarah-barns/" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">get in touch</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="sov-footer">
      <p>
        <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/a504252cf109454598a7b02e5024b10d" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Report a missing site, an error, or contestation not yet recorded.</a>
        Other tools are on the <a href="/doing/">Do</a> page.
      </p>
    </footer>

  </main>

  <style>
    .sov {
      padding: 3rem 2rem 4rem;
      max-width: 880px;
    }

    .sov-header {
      margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
      padding-bottom: 2rem;
      border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
    }

    .sov-eyebrow {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    }

    .sov-header h1 {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
      font-weight: 300;
      line-height: 1.08;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    }

    .sov-intro {
      font-family: var(--body);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--text-dim);
      max-width: 62ch;
    }

    .sov-wayfind {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 0.6rem;
      margin-top: 1.2rem;
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
      color: var(--text-faint);
    }

    .sov-wayfind a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 4px;
    }

    .sov-intro a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
      font-style: normal;
    }

    .sov-note {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-top: 1rem;
      max-width: 65ch;
    }

    .sov-note a {
      color: var(--text-dim);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    .sov-note a:hover {
      color: var(--purple);
    }

    .sov-section {
      margin-top: 3rem;
    }

    .sov-section h2 {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2rem);
      font-weight: 400;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
      margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    }

    .sov-section p {
      font-family: var(--body);
      font-size: 1rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
      color: var(--text);
      max-width: 65ch;
      margin-bottom: 1.35rem;
    }

    .sov-section p a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    .sov-footer {
      margin-top: 3.5rem;
      padding-top: 2rem;
      border-top: 2px solid var(--border);
    }

    .sov-footer p {
      font-family: var(--body);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      color: var(--text-dim);
      max-width: 60ch;
    }

    .sov-footer a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    .sov-chart {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      height: auto;
      margin-top: 1.25rem;
      border: 2px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--border);
      background: #fafafa;
    }

    .sov-figure {
      margin: 1.5rem 0 1.75rem;
    }

    /* The diagram is wider than the reading column: on desktop it breaks out
       to the page width so its labels stay legible. */
    @media (min-width: 1000px) {
      .sov-figure {
        width: min(1180px, calc(100vw - 4rem));
        position: relative;
        left: 50%;
        transform: translateX(-50%);
      }
    }

    .sov-diagram {
      border: 2px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--border);
      background: var(--bg);
      padding: 0.75rem 0.5rem 0.25rem;
      overflow-x: auto;
    }

    .sov-diagram :global(svg) {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      min-width: 760px;
      height: auto;
      font-family: var(--heading);
    }

    .sov-figure figcaption {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-top: 0.8rem;
    }

    .sov-figure figcaption a {
      color: var(--purple);
      margin-left: 0.4rem;
    }

    .sov-section p.sov-cite {
      border: 2px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--border);
      background: var(--tint-purple-a);
      padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    }

    .sov-section ul.sov-registers {
      margin: -0.35rem 0 1.35rem 1.25rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
      font-family: var(--body);
      max-width: 62ch;
    }

    .sov-section ul.sov-registers li {
      margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
    }

    .sov-section p strong,
    .sov-section li strong {
      font-weight: 700;
      color: var(--text);
    }

    .sov-figure-chart .sov-diagram :global(svg) {
      min-width: 640px;
    }

    .sov-section ul.sov-deps {
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
      gap: 0.75rem;
    }

    .sov-section ul.sov-deps li:first-child {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }

    .sov-section ul.sov-deps li {
      border: 2px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      background: var(--tint-terracotta-a);
      padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
    }

    .sov-section ul.sov-deps li strong {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      color: var(--ink-terracotta);
    }

    .sov-section p.sov-chart-credit {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-top: 0.8rem;
    }

    .sov-section p.sov-chart-credit a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    .sov-cost {
      margin-top: 3rem;
      border: 2px solid var(--border);
      border-left: 4px solid #D16D54;
      background: #faf6f0;
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--border);
      padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    }

    .sov-cost-label {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .sov-cost p:last-child {
      font-family: var(--body);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
      color: var(--text);
      max-width: 65ch;
      margin: 0;
    }

    .sov-cost a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      .sov {
        padding: 2rem 1rem 3rem;
      }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
